{
  "id": "murphy-shakti8-feelgood-2phase-60min",
  "name": "8 Coil Shakti Feelgood: temporal, then frontal (60 min)",
  "category": "mood",
  "evidence": "claim",
  "origin": "Todd Murphy (8 Coil Shakti), design based on Baker-Price & Persinger's burst-firing studies; signal templates licensed from Persinger & Koren",
  "uT": 1,
  "summary": "Murphy's two-phase Feelgood session for the 8 Coil Shakti: the amygdala and hippocampal signals one after the other with 4-s pauses, first over both temporal lobes, then over both frontal lobes. The vendor presents it for 'overcoming fear, anger and sadness' and says it suits people who are 'at their worst in the morning'.",
  "notes": "60-minute version (30 + 30 min, switching abruptly); the vendor also offers 20-min phases (murphy-shakti8-feelgood-2phase-40min) and says to begin with the one-phase frontal session (murphy-shakti8-feelgood-1phase-frontal). The vendor: 'the left stereo channel applies the signals to the temporal lobes, and the right stereo channel applies the signals to the frontal lobes'. Here CH1-4 sit over the temporal lobes (FT7, TP7, FT8, TP8: two coils per side around T7/T8) and CH5-8 over the frontal lobes (Fp1, AF3, Fp2, AF4); the exact sites are an inference from vendor photos. All coils of the active group carry the signal at once, with no rotation and no left/right alternation (the 2007 version of the page: the signals 'repeat for 30 minutes through only one channel ... After 30 minutes, when the left channel is silenced and the right begins'). Polarity: the Shakti's coils come as + and - ('You need TWO poles (positive and negative) to make ONE magnetic field'), one of each per stereo channel on each side. As in murphy-shakti8-altered-states-40min, which uses the same eight coils, Fp1 and AF4 run as is and AF3 and Fp2 reversed (weight -1), after the vendor's front-view photos, and on the temples TP7 and FT8 run as is and FT7 and TP8 reversed, after the vendor's coil chart (its side-view photos disagree: the two on the Feelgood consent page show the front coil + and the back coil -, a 2021 view on the Mood enhancement page the reverse). The vendor calls phase 1 'preparation for the second phase': if unwanted effects come in phase 1 'you can skip to the second, or skip the first part altogether', but stopping in phase 1 'may leave you further from the goal of the session than you were before'. The vendor's signals are proprietary. One pass here is: the amygdala signal, approximated by the built-in accelerating burst (6 to 30 Hz, 230 points of 3 ms = 0.69 s; the 230-point length is from Ryczko & Persinger 2002); 4 s of silence (the vendor: 'generated one after the other ... with a four-second latency'); the hippocampal 'LTP' signal, approximated by four theta bursts of 4 pulses at 100 Hz (the Larson & Lynch timing of the project's ltp generator) at the 5.4 Hz spectral peak the vendor reports for it (0.6 s, with 1-ms points instead of the vendor's 3-ms points so the 100 Hz pulses fit); then 4 s of silence again. That gives about 8 s of field per minute (the vendor states 9.6 s). The vendor says the effect is 'like aspirin' (little change if you already feel good) and is often noticed only the next morning. The vendor warns that too many Feelgood (frontal) or Altered States (temporal) sessions 'can create a kind of malaise or even lethargy'; if unwanted effects appear after more than one series over one area, it suggests moving to the other. Intensity: the Shakti software defaults to 50% volume, which the vendor calls the 50 mG 'laboratory standard' (5 µT), while its measurement page gives 10 mG (1 µT) as the normal peak; no independent measurement of a Shakti unit was found, and this file uses 1 µT. Persinger's lab reported better mood with weekly burst-firing fields in small samples (Baker-Price & Persinger 1996, 2003, the latter with temporal or left-frontal placement; Tsang et al. 2009); none has been independently replicated. An independent randomized sham-controlled test of the 8 Coil Shakti amygdala signal over the temporal lobes found no effect on emotional responses (Gendle & McGrath 2012; Murphy argues it used the wrong session), and a double-blind God Helmet test found no field effect (Granqvist 2005). The same lab reported more aggression after three weekly bilateral burst-firing sessions (Gillis & Persinger 1993) and more acceptance of false statements with burst-firing over the left prefrontal region (Ross, Koren & Persinger 2008). Not a treatment for depression or any illness: the vendor excludes people with psychiatric diagnoses. Not for people with epilepsy; stop if distressed. Revised after a re-check of the vendor's Feelgood pages (2026): coil polarity added (all coils had the same polarity before), and the 'not in sensory deprivation' rule, the per-length schedule and the vendor's intensity figures noted; on review, the time-of-day, interruption and snapback statements were corrected to the vendor's wording and its malaise caution added. Still approximate: the coil sites, the temporal polarity and both signal templates.",
  "setting": "Not in sensory deprivation: the vendor says the Feelgood session 'should not be done in sensory deprivation', since it is meant for 'outward-looking', 'this-worldly' effects. No time of day is prescribed (the vendor's page on weekly sessions says they 'can be done any time of day'); the vendor notes that the effect often shows only the morning after.",
  "schedule": "Vendor: no more often than once a week when effects appear for the 60-min session (every three days for the 40-min one); six sessions, then a 3-week break. The vendor's 60-minute page exempts two-phase sessions such as this one from its limit of one hour-long session per three weeks. If no effects appear, alternate Feelgood and Altered States (murphy-shakti8-altered-states-60min) every three days for three sessions, then stop for a week (the vendor's general safety page, by contrast, advises against going back and forth between two session designs). If a session is interrupted before it ends, wait three days and start it again from the beginning (if you miss a session of an every-3-days series, the vendor says to stop for three weeks). The vendor's schedule pages describe a possible 'metabolic snapback' beginning about four days after the second session; the vendor says the effects of one session last 3-4 days, and claims a 6-week series lasts about 6 months.",
  "targets": ["left-amygdala", "right-amygdala", "left-hippocampus-head", "right-hippocampus-head", "vmpfc", "left-dlpfc", "right-dlpfc"],
